issues.quote:
**Defect — pagination repeats the last row of each full page.** The contract says the cursor identifies the last emitted row, so the next page must select rows strictly greater than the cursor. In `query.mjs`, `x >= cursor` includes that row again; `page.mjs` then returns the last selected item as the next cursor.

A reproducible witness, assuming ascending input:

```js
import { page } from './page.mjs';

const first = page([1, 2, 3], undefined, 2);
// Predicted: { items: [1, 2], cursor: 2 }

const second = page([1, 2, 3], first.cursor, 2);
// Predicted: { items: [2, 3], cursor: 3 }
```

Row `2` appears twice, violating the exactly-once obligation. With size `1`, repeated calls can remain stuck on the same row indefinitely. The predicate should use `x > cursor` when a cursor is present.

These are source-based predictions; no checks were executed. The initial-page branch is protected against this particular defect because an omitted cursor includes all rows before slicing.

