The governing local contract requires every batch increment to be a finite integer in 0..10 and requires validation of the whole batch before account state changes. Only apply.mjs is authorized for changes.

Defect — apply.mjs accepts NaN, fractional numbers, and non-number values that pass its comparison checks; for example, apply({balance:0}, [2, NaN]) completes and sets balance to NaN. The supplied tests check valid integers and out-of-range integers, but do not protect the finite-integer requirement (T1, T5 warning; test dimension owns this finding). A reproducible witness is `import assert from 'node:assert/strict'; import {apply} from './apply.mjs'; const s={balance:0}; assert.throws(()=>apply(s,[2,NaN])); assert.equal(s.balance,0);` The first assertion is predicted to fail against the supplied source. A replacement validates every item with Number.isFinite and Number.isInteger and the inclusive bounds before the mutation loop. This preserves the existing valid-number behavior; callers that relied on coercion or non-integers will now receive an error, as required by the contract.

Protected — existing.test.mjs covers the supplied integer range boundaries and verifies no partial mutation for batches containing 11 or -1. The pre-existing legacy.test.mjs failure and user-note.txt are preserved.

